  {
   "id": 28,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    87.39013620968251,
    -406.9243358895653
   ],
   "size": [
    1166.7010369858165,
    292.59177695771245
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 0,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## wan-animate-mix.json\n\nPut YOUR character into footage, keeping the footage\n\n**Does:** character replacement (WanAnimateToVideo **Mix mode**) - a driving clip of a person in a scene + a **portrait** reference of our character -> the character plays that person's part, background and lighting kept. This is placement: the character lands *inside* real footage, not on a generated backdrop. Host: `wan2.2_animate_14B_int8_convrot` (18.4 GB, **Apache-2.0**, already on the box) + the lightx2v distill lora + our usual umt5/VAE/clip_vision.\n**Measured (E13c, 2026-10-11):** 77 frames at 640x640 in **375 s**; 33 frames at 848x480 in **240 s** - DWPose preprocessing included, so this is the *cheap* 14B route (the 6-step / cfg 1.0 distill recipe). Identity holds with a **portrait** reference; probe B proved a full-body reference loses the face on close-up-driven footage (same outfit, different woman).\n**Knobs:** the **reference image** (portrait! that is the identity knob); the **mask prompt** (`person` on `SAM3_Detect`) and `GrowMask`/`BlockifyMask` (10 / 32) if the driver's person is unusual; `length` (77 ships); the **prompt** describes the scene action, not the character (the reference already carries identity). Motion comes from `pose_video` (DWPose hand+body) and `face_video` (DWPose face), `reference_image_strength`-style tightening is not exposed on this node.\n**Traps:** the socket order is `positive, negative, vae, clip_vision_output, reference_image, face_video, pose_video, background_video, character_mask` - `reference_image` sits AFTER `clip_vision_output`. The mask and the video must share the canvas (a mismatched mask shows up as black 32-px blocks - measured). The output prepends the reference as frame 0 (81 frames out for `length` 77) - trim one second in the engine or with a slice. DWPose auto-downloads its detectors on first run (`custom_nodes/comfyui_controlnet_aux/ckpts/`). The relight lora ships in the chain (lightx2v -> relight - the official template includes it): measured on a\n    neutral-lit room the integration difference is subtle (subject-vs-background palette mismatch 27.6 -> 25.5\n    across matched frames, E13d) - on strongly-lit scenes it should matter more; re-measure if a composite\n    reads pasted.\n**Siblings:** `wan-animate-motion.json` (S15) is the Move mode - motion transfer onto a generated backdrop; this flow keeps the *footage's* background instead.\n\n---\n*How to run it: story **S31** in `HOWTO.md`  \u00b7  Why it is this way: **E13c** in `video/EXPERIMENTS.md`.*\n\n**size - edit ONLY the two `INT Constant` nodes** (multiples of 16): they drive the `ImageScale` hub, `WanAnimateToVideo`'s canvas, and both `DWPreprocessor` map resolutions. Sources can be any size - the hub normalizes them (center-cropped to the canvas aspect, not stretched). The shipped 640x640 square is the tested configuration; cost proportional to pixels - the 6-step LoRA keeps it cheap, but pixels still bite."
   ],
   "widgets_values_named": {
    "text": "## wan-animate-mix.json\n\nPut YOUR character into footage, keeping the footage\n\n**Does:** character replacement (WanAnimateToVideo **Mix mode**) - a driving clip of a person in a scene + a **portrait** reference of our character -> the character plays that person's part, background and lighting kept. This is placement: the character lands *inside* real footage, not on a generated backdrop. Host: `wan2.2_animate_14B_int8_convrot` (18.4 GB, **Apache-2.0**, already on the box) + the lightx2v distill lora + our usual umt5/VAE/clip_vision.\n**Measured (E13c, 2026-10-11):** 77 frames at 640x640 in **375 s**; 33 frames at 848x480 in **240 s** - DWPose preprocessing included, so this is the *cheap* 14B route (the 6-step / cfg 1.0 distill recipe). Identity holds with a **portrait** reference; probe B proved a full-body reference loses the face on close-up-driven footage (same outfit, different woman).\n**Knobs:** the **reference image** (portrait! that is the identity knob); the **mask prompt** (`person` on `SAM3_Detect`) and `GrowMask`/`BlockifyMask` (10 / 32) if the driver's person is unusual; `length` (77 ships); the **prompt** describes the scene action, not the character (the reference already carries identity). Motion comes from `pose_video` (DWPose hand+body) and `face_video` (DWPose face), `reference_image_strength`-style tightening is not exposed on this node.\n**Traps:** the socket order is `positive, negative, vae, clip_vision_output, reference_image, face_video, pose_video, background_video, character_mask` - `reference_image` sits AFTER `clip_vision_output`. The mask and the video must share the canvas (a mismatched mask shows up as black 32-px blocks - measured). The output prepends the reference as frame 0 (81 frames out for `length` 77) - trim one second in the engine or with a slice. DWPose auto-downloads its detectors on first run (`custom_nodes/comfyui_controlnet_aux/ckpts/`). The relight lora ships in the chain (lightx2v -> relight - the official template includes it): measured on a\n    neutral-lit room the integration difference is subtle (subject-vs-background palette mismatch 27.6 -> 25.5\n    across matched frames, E13d) - on strongly-lit scenes it should matter more; re-measure if a composite\n    reads pasted.\n**Siblings:** `wan-animate-motion.json` (S15) is the Move mode - motion transfer onto a generated backdrop; this flow keeps the *footage's* background instead.\n\n---\n*How to run it: story **S31** in `HOWTO.md`  \u00b7  Why it is this way: **E13c** in `video/EXPERIMENTS.md`.*\n\n**size - edit ONLY the two `INT Constant` nodes** (multiples of 16): they drive the `ImageScale` hub, `WanAnimateToVideo`'s canvas, and both `DWPreprocessor` map resolutions. Sources can be any size - the hub normalizes them (center-cropped to the canvas aspect, not stretched). The shipped 640x640 square is the tested configuration; cost proportional to pixels - the 6-step LoRA keeps it cheap, but pixels still bite."
   },
